## Summary

Running the signed-in smoke flows meant typing the test account's email and password on every run, and passing them on the command line. Failures could only be read by opening `~/.maestro/tests/` by hand. This makes a run one command and keeps the evidence.

- **`app/.maestro/smoke.sh`**, now what `npm run smoke:ios` runs:
  - checks that Maestro is installed, finds Java 17+, and checks that Metro answers (`/status`);
  - reads the test account from the macOS Keychain (service `citycrew-maestro`);
  - runs Maestro with a JUnit report and full debug output into `.smoke-local/maestro/<timestamp>/`, with `latest →` pointing at the newest run;
  - never prints the password;
  - passes a flow path through to run just that flow;
  - takes `EXPO_URL`, `TEST_EMAIL` and `TEST_PASSWORD` from the environment when set.
- **`.gitignore`** gains `.smoke-local/`: bundles, PR drafts and run results are machine-local, and a failure screenshot can show a signed-in account.
- **README**: setup stores the account in the Keychain once, running is `npm run smoke:ios`, and failures are read from `latest/`.
- **`GUIDELINES.md`** (new): the approach behind the suite and 12 rules for writing a flow, each drawn from a failure this suite has already had (tab bar ducking, bottom-of-page buttons, `centerElement`, cleanup, ids per state, secrets), plus a checklist for adding a flow.

No app code changes.

## Test plan

- [x] `bash -n` and `shellcheck` are clean.
- [x] Script run against stubbed `security`, `curl` and `maestro`:
  - no Metro → clear message, exit 1;
  - no Keychain entry → setup hint, exit 1;
  - normal run → the right args, a report, a `latest` link, exit 0;
  - one flow with a Maestro failure → exit 1;
  - the password never appears in the output or in `console.log`.
- [x] `maestroIds.test.ts` and lint are green.
- [ ] On the Mac: `security add-generic-password -s citycrew-maestro -a <email> -w`, then `npm run smoke:ios` runs all seven flows green, and `.smoke-local/maestro/latest/report.xml` exists.

